## 2. Current System State & Architecture Map

Confidence: ✅ High — recently verified in this session.

Implemented:

- `src/files.ts` bounds and extracts local searchable PDF/text input.
- `src/analyze.ts` classifies one proposal, extracts issued IDs, preserves snippets, detects
  duplicates, and produces `ACKNOWLEDGED` or `REVIEW`.
- `src/report.ts` creates the local Markdown evidence download.
- `src/main.ts` owns selection, progress, friendly recovery, safe DOM output, and download state.
- `src/styles.css` owns the mobile-first one-screen hierarchy and automatic appearance.
- `tests/` covers pure logic, real PDF extraction, the browser workflow, accessibility, keyboard,
  failures, overflow, appearance, and responsive screenshots.

Flow:

```text
local selected files
  -> input bounds
  -> local PDF/text extraction
  -> proposal scoring and ambiguity stop
  -> issued identifier extraction
  -> contextual proposal match
  -> source-linked ACKNOWLEDGED or REVIEW
  -> local Markdown download
```

Broken or flaky product behavior: none known. Public source publication is verified at
`https://github.com/ownasquare/addendum-echo`, with public visibility, default branch `main`, and
equal local/remote publisher readback. Registry and lane closeout remain pending at the time of
this evidence update.

## 3. Critical Decisions & Reasoning History

Confidence: ✅ High — recently verified in this session.

- Narrowed the job to visible identifier evidence because full bid responsiveness depends on legal
  and solicitation context.
- Used deterministic local rules because this transformation needs reproducibility and no paid or
  credentialed provider.
- Stops on an equal proposal-score tie instead of adding a selector or guessing.
- Keeps generic “all addenda” language at `REVIEW`; exact IDs are the product promise.
- Defers OCR because it adds a second probabilistic confidence system and violates the smallest
  complete product target.
- Keeps screenshots outside Git; the repeatable Playwright matrix is the durable proof.

